msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:89
msgid "Some Odoo fields do not only store a value: writing them also writes into a **different** model. ``crm.lead.email_from``, for example, updates the linked partner's email address. Left unguarded, an assistant allowed to \"edit leads\" could change contact data outside the models you opened, and the audit log would only name the lead."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:95
msgid "Odoo has two kinds of such field, and only one of them can be opened."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:97
msgid "A **related** field re-routes the write to the other model by definition. The gate refuses it outright, on create and on write, and no access rule of any shape opens it — allow the assistant to write the *target* model instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:101
msgid "A field carrying an **inverse** runs arbitrary code that may touch anything: ``crm.lead.email_from`` is compute-plus-inverse, and its inverse writes ``res.partner.email``. The gate refuses such a field **unless a field-level rule explicitly allows writing that exact field**. A model-level Allow is deliberately *not* enough: granting write on ``crm.lead`` must not silently open ``res.partner`` as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:108
msgid "The surface is wider than it looks. Core Odoo carries inverses on everyday fields — a vendor bill's partner, journal, currency and payment term, an invoice line's product and account, a lead's email — so expect to meet this rail on the first real write you enable."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:113
msgid "Either refusal is a configuration boundary, not abuse: it records no violation and costs the user no strike."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:117
msgid "Opening one of these fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:119
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Security --> Access Rules`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:120
msgid "Create a rule with :guilabel:`Model` **and** :guilabel:`Field` set — the field is what makes it count."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:122
msgid "Set :guilabel:`perm_write` = **Allow**."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:125
msgid "A rule with :guilabel:`Field` left empty is a model-level rule and will not lift this rail, however permissive it looks. :guilabel:`perm_write` is the permission that counts, and it is also what opens the field inside a *create* payload: :guilabel:`perm_create` is model-level and is ignored on field rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:130
msgid "The rule lifts the inverse rail and nothing else. A field that is not stored in the database — ``product.template.standard_price`` is computed with an inverse but has no column — and a related field both stay refused however permissive the rule is. So if the refusal survives a correct field-level rule, the field is related or not stored, not an inverse field."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:137
msgid "When the refusal happens inside a one2many or many2many payload — an invoice line inside :guilabel:`Invoice lines`, for example — the assistant reports only the **parent** field, ``invoice_line_ids``, because that is the field it tried to write. The nested model and field that were actually refused go to the Odoo **server** log at INFO level; search it for ``AI x2many write refused``. They do not appear under :menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Logs`, and a refusal for a missing field opt-in records no violation either — it is a configuration boundary, not abuse."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:147
msgid "Defaults and recommended postures"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:150
msgid "Fresh install"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:152
msgid "Read: **allow** (minus floor and strips) so the assistant is useful for Q&A."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:153
msgid "Create / write / delete: **deny** until you open specific models."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:154
msgid "Ten field-level write allows ship enabled, so the vendor-bill and invoice-line flows keep working under the cross-model rail above: ``account.move`` (partner, journal, currency, payment term, delivery date, payment reference) and ``account.move.line`` (product, account, analytic distribution, partner). Nothing is opened on ``crm.lead``."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:160
msgid "``account.move.line.analytic_distribution`` is the one to read twice. It is a JSON field, so its rule lifts two rails at once: the cross-model rail above and the refusal of JSON-typed values, which no other shipped rule touches. Its inverse also leaves the document — on a **posted** line it deletes the line's ``account.analytic.line`` records and re-creates them — so an analytic write reaches a model no rule ever named and no audit entry names either."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:167
msgid "These rows carry **no access group and no company**, so they are global rules: they apply to every AI user and every agent, not only to the invoicing flow. They arrive on install **and** on every upgrade of the AI app. If you edit or deactivate one, an upgrade will not overwrite your change; if you delete one, it comes back."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:173
msgid "They exist only where **Accounting** is installed, and each install or upgrade of the AI app seeds only the apps present at that moment. Install Accounting *after* the AI app and the rows appear only at the next upgrade of the AI app — until then the vendor-bill flow refuses invoice-line writes with no other symptom, so upgrade the AI app after adding Accounting."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:179
msgid "Two model-level allows ship enabled as well, on the two explicit-allow-only models of step 5: ``ir.attachment`` is opened for read and write (PDF and image content for the document and vendor-bill skills, and re-attaching a file to another record), ``mail.activity`` for read, write and create (review To-Dos authored by the AI user). Creating or deleting an attachment and deleting an activity stay closed. Like the field rows they are global, arrive on install and on every upgrade, and survive your edits."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:188
msgid "On the nested-line path the four ``account.move.line`` rows are real write permission, not only a lifted rail. A field-level Allow is resolved before the model tier and before the global default, so once write is allowed on ``account.move``, the assistant can set product, account, analytic distribution and partner on invoice lines through :guilabel:`Invoice lines` even though write on ``account.move.line`` itself was never allowed — the create/write default of Deny does not hold that line. A *direct* write to ``account.move.line`` is still refused; only a payload nested inside a parent field skips the model-level check."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:198
msgid "Header fields on ``account.move`` are unaffected: writing the document still needs your own model-level Allow. To close the four line fields, add an explicit **Deny** — model-level on ``account.move.line``, or on the field — or deactivate the shipped rules. A Deny at either tier beats the Allow, and either edit survives every upgrade; deleting a shipped rule only brings it back."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:205
msgid "Deny-by-default read (stricter)"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:207
msgid "Set default read to **Deny**, then add Allow rules only for models the assistant should see (e.g. ``product.product``, ``res.partner`` with field denies on internal notes, etc.). Safer for regulated databases; requires more setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:212
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:215
msgid "Deny all AI read on employees"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:217
msgid "Create global rule on model ``hr.employee``."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:218
msgid "Set :guilabel:`perm_read` = **Deny** (other perms inherit/deny as you like)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:221
msgid "Allow support agent to create helpdesk tickets only"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:223
msgid "Create AI access group *Support bot* with the agent user as member."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:224
msgid "Global defaults already deny create."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:225
