#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:3
msgid "Who can use what"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:5
msgid "This page is an overview for administrators: which group, capability and rule each AI feature depends on, and where to change it. The principle is always the same: AI works with your rights and can only be narrowed, never widened."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:10
msgid "The three layers"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:12
msgid "Groups. :guilabel:`AI: User` lets a person use the assistant and the one-click features. :guilabel:`AI: Administrator` lets a person configure providers, policies, agents and sources. Both are set on the user form under the AI category."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:16
msgid "Capabilities. Master switches under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Behavior --> Capabilities` decide which classes of tools exist at all: :guilabel:`Ask (Q&A)`, :guilabel:`Read data`, :guilabel:`Navigate UI`, :guilabel:`Write data`, :guilabel:`Delete data`, :guilabel:`Web access`, :guilabel:`MCP tools`, :guilabel:`Business actions`, :guilabel:`Customize UI & schema` and :guilabel:`Guided system setup`. Write, delete, web, MCP, actions and customization are off after installation. A capability can be limited to groups."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:24
msgid "Access rules. Under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Security --> Access Rules`, per-model and per-field allow and deny rules sit on top of Odoo's own rights. By default reading is allowed and creating, writing and deleting are denied. See :doc:`access_policy`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:30
msgid "What each feature needs"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:32
msgid "AI panel, writing assistant, AI search, command palette, dictation: :guilabel:`AI: User`, a chat model, the :guilabel:`Ask (Q&A)` capability. The assistant sees record data only if :guilabel:`Read data` and the access rules allow reading that model for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:36
msgid "Changes made by the assistant in chat: :guilabel:`Write data` or :guilabel:`Delete data`, plus an allow rule for the model and fields, plus the write mode. Keep confirmation on."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:39
msgid "One-click buttons in apps (assign a lead, describe a product, draft timesheets, file a document): the click is your decision, so they do not need the write capability, but they read data through the same access rules. If the AI is not allowed to read the model, you get a clear message instead of a result."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:44
msgid "AI fields and AI server actions: the user chosen in :guilabel:`Acts as` (or the agent task's agent user). See :doc:`fields_and_automation`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:46
msgid "Knowledge sources: sources are managed by AI administrators; each person only gets excerpts from sources and records they may read themselves (:doc:`knowledge_sources`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:49
msgid "MCP server: internal users in :guilabel:`AI: User`; only an allow-list of tools; changes always wait for your confirmation (:doc:`mcp_server`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:51
msgid "Live chat agent: a dedicated service user that can only ask, read the data sources you list and hand over; it gets no write rights beyond creating a lead, if you turn that on (:doc:`livechat`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:54
msgid "Website builder tools: :guilabel:`Customize UI & schema` and the website designer group; every change is a confirmation card (:doc:`website`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:56
msgid "AI triage of project tasks: reviewer in :guilabel:`AI: User`, the :guilabel:`Write data` capability and an allow rule for writing the task fields it changes (:doc:`project_sla`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:61
msgid "Limits and traces"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:63
msgid "Rate and cost limits per person and per day are under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings` (see :doc:`configuration`). Repeated policy violations earn strikes and a temporary ban."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:66
msgid "Every model call is written to the AI log and every applied change to the change log, both under :menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Logs` and :menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Mutation Logs` (see :doc:`monitoring`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/feature_access.rst:71
msgid "Roll out in steps: start with the assistant and the writing features for a pilot group, add knowledge sources, then enable one write path at a time and read the logs for a couple of weeks before widening it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:3
msgid "AI fields, server actions and automations"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:5
msgid "AI can fill in data for you without anybody opening the assistant: a field that is written by the model, a server action that asks the model for a value, or a server action that hands a record to an agent. This page is for administrators."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:10
msgid "AI fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:12
msgid "An AI field is an existing, stored and editable field of any model that the model fills from an instruction. Typical examples: a one-sentence summary of a request, the category that fits a product best, a priority class. Supported field types are text, rich text, integer, decimal, monetary, checkbox, selection, date, date and time, many2one and many2many."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:18
msgid "Create one under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Behavior --> AI Fields`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:21
msgid "Choose the model and the field. A field can have only one AI rule."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:22
msgid "Write the instruction, for example \"A one-sentence summary of what the customer asks for.\""
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:24
msgid ":guilabel:`Acts as` is the user whose access rights are used to read the record and to write the value. Choose a user who may read and change that model, never a more powerful one than needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:27
msgid "Restrict the records with the domain, and use :guilabel:`Also show these fields` to give the model related fields to look at. For a relational field, the choice domain limits which records the model may pick."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:32
msgid "When values are filled"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:34
msgid ":guilabel:`Auto Fill`: a scheduled action runs every 15 minutes and fills only empty values, newest records first, in batches (20 records by default, system parameter ``ai.fields.batch_size``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:37
msgid ":guilabel:`Fill empty values now` on the rule runs it at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:38
msgid "Numbers and checkboxes have no \"empty\" state, so they are only filled from the :guilabel:`Fill AI fields` entry of the Actions menu, which appears automatically on the model while a rule exists, or from an automation. Tick :guilabel:`Overwrite` if that action should also replace values that are already set."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:44
msgid "The model must answer with a value that is valid for the field: selection keys, related records among the offered choices and dates are checked before anything is written, and an answer of \"nothing\" leaves the field empty. The record is shown to the model without the fields the assistant may never read. Every write is made as the :guilabel:`Acts as` user, so Odoo access rights and record rules apply, and it appears in the AI change log under :menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Mutation Logs`. Problems are kept in the rule (:guilabel:`Last run` and the last error)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:54
msgid "AI in server actions"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:56
msgid "Two kinds of server action use AI. They need :doc:`developer mode <../../general/developer_mode>` to edit under :menuselection:`Settings --> Technical --> Server Actions`, and they can also be used as actions of an automation rule."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:61
msgid ":guilabel:`Ask AI`: on an \"Update Record\" action, set the value type to :guilabel:`Ask AI` and describe in :guilabel:`AI instruction` what the model should put in the field (\"Describe what the AI should put in the field...\"). For each record the field is set from the model's answer, using the same engine and the same checks as AI fields. It works on saved records only."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:66
msgid ":guilabel:`Hand to an AI agent`: choose an :guilabel:`Agent task` and the records are queued as work items of that standing task. A background job then runs one isolated agent run per record with the task's agent, capabilities, instruction and write mode. Only agents with their own user and a supervisor can be used, because unattended work must never run in the name of whoever triggered it. See :doc:`agents` and the work-item sections there."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:74
msgid "Automations"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:76
msgid "Combine a trigger with one of those actions in :menuselection:`Settings --> Technical --> Automation --> Automation Rules`: for example \"when a task is created, hand it to the triage agent\" or \"when a lead is created, fill its category with AI\". Time-based work (\"every Monday, summarise last week's tickets\") is better modelled as a standing agent task with a schedule under :menuselection:`AI --> Agents --> Tasks`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:83
msgid "Administrators with the :guilabel:`Customize UI & schema` capability can also ask the assistant in chat to list, create, change or run automations. Creating or changing one is always a proposal that needs confirmation."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/fields_and_automation.rst:88
msgid "Unattended AI acts without anyone clicking. Keep the model and field lists narrow, choose a low-privilege :guilabel:`Acts as` user, keep agent tasks on confirmation write mode unless the work is low risk, and review the AI change log regularly."
msgstr ""

