#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:3
msgid "AI in Knowledge, Documents and cloud files"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:5
msgid "Three small modules connect the AI to the places where your company keeps its articles and files. They build on the knowledge sources described in :doc:`knowledge_sources`, so the same indexing, citation and access rules apply, and they install themselves when the required apps are present. AI Knowledge needs the Knowledge app, AI Documents needs the Documents app and AI Cloud Storage needs cloud storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:13
msgid "The pgvector extension and an embedding model are required for all knowledge sources (see :doc:`knowledge_sources`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:17
msgid "Knowledge articles as sources"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:19
msgid "In :menuselection:`AI --> Knowledge --> Sources`, create a source and choose the type :guilabel:`Knowledge articles`. Pick one or more :guilabel:`Knowledge spaces` (every article of the space is used) and/or single :guilabel:`Articles`. :guilabel:`Include sub-articles` is on by default. At least one space or article is needed. Templates and articles in the trash are not indexed, and a source covers at most 2000 articles by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:26
msgid "Each article is indexed together with its position in the space (space, parent articles, title), so a search finds it by context. A citation chip in an answer opens the article."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:29
msgid "When an article changes, only that article is indexed again, at the next indexing run (every 10 minutes) or when you click :guilabel:`Index now`. Moving an article takes its sub-articles along; deleting an article removes its excerpts at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:33
msgid "The Knowledge app's own sharing is respected for every question: a private, archived or trashed article is never cited to someone who may not see it, even before it is indexed again. Files embedded in an article are not indexed with it; add them as a file source if you need them."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:39
msgid "Draft an article with AI"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:41
msgid "In the article editor, the top bar shows :guilabel:`Draft with AI` when a main AI model is configured, you are an AI user and the article is not locked or in the trash. It saves the article and opens a dialog with three steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:45
msgid ":guilabel:`Brief`: describe :guilabel:`What should the article cover?` (the title is the default). If knowledge sources are available to you, you can switch on :guilabel:`Use the knowledge sources` so that the AI draws on your company's own material. Click :guilabel:`Propose an outline`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:49
msgid ":guilabel:`Outline`: the AI proposes 3 to 8 sections as a list. Edit it freely, or click :guilabel:`New outline` to ask again. Then click :guilabel:`Write the article`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:52
msgid ":guilabel:`Preview`: read the finished article. Click :guilabel:`Insert into the article`, or :guilabel:`Write again` or go back. When the article already has text, choose :guilabel:`When accepting` between :guilabel:`Replace the current content` and :guilabel:`Add after the current content`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:57
msgid "The AI sees the title, the space, the current content and the names of the parent and sibling articles that you may see, and the excerpts from the sources when you chose them. It is told not to invent company facts and to put placeholders in square brackets, so review and complete the draft. The text goes into the article with your rights, is recorded in the AI change log and counts as a normal edit in the article history."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:65
msgid "AI cover"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:67
msgid "When an image model is configured (see :doc:`models_and_providers`), a picture button in the top bar (tooltip :guilabel:`Generate a cover image with AI`) opens a dialog with an editable description of the cover (by default a calm, wide banner for the title, without text). Click :guilabel:`Generate`, look at the preview, and then :guilabel:`Use as cover`, try again or :guilabel:`Change the description`. The cover appears above the article."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:75
msgid "Document folders as sources"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:77
msgid "In :menuselection:`AI --> Knowledge --> Sources`, choose the type :guilabel:`Document folders`, pick the folders and decide with :guilabel:`Include sub-folders` whether the sub-folders count. Files attached to fields and links are not used. A source covers at most 500 documents by default, newest first, and the text of each file is read up to 25 MB. When a document is attached to a business record, its excerpts also follow the record's access rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:85
msgid "New and changed documents are indexed again incrementally. At question time a document is used only if you may read its folder and the document itself, with the same visibility as in the Documents app. Users without the Documents app get nothing from these sources."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:91
msgid "AI filing of documents"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:93
msgid "The AI can propose the right folder and tags for a document, either when you ask or when a file is uploaded."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:97
msgid "On request"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:99
msgid "On a document form, click :guilabel:`Suggest a folder` (you need write access to the document). The proposal is computed at once and shows the folder, the tags, the confidence and the reason. Click :guilabel:`Apply` or :guilabel:`Dismiss`. A pending proposal also appears as a banner on the document."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:105
msgid "On upload"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:107
msgid "Open a folder and look at the group :guilabel:`AI filing`. Choose :guilabel:`Suggest a folder` so that every document uploaded into the folder gets a proposal that the uploader confirms, or :guilabel:`File automatically` so that proposals are applied at once when the AI is at least as sure as the :guilabel:`Minimum confidence` (0.8 by default); the other proposals wait for confirmation. The option :guilabel:`AI may file documents here` decides whether other documents can be filed into this folder. Proposals are prepared about every 10 minutes, in the name of the person who uploaded the file, with that person's rights."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:117
msgid "Open :menuselection:`Documents --> AI filing` to see the proposals (the filter :guilabel:`To decide` is on by default), with the filters :guilabel:`Applied`, :guilabel:`Failed` and :guilabel:`Applied automatically`. A failed proposal can be retried with :guilabel:`Try again`. When a proposal is applied, the previous folder and tags are kept on it, and a note in the document chatter says who confirmed it, or that it was filed automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:125
msgid "The AI reads the first part of a document's text only when the AI read policy allows documents, or when the folder has AI filing turned on, and never when an explicit AI deny rule applies to you. Filing runs with the uploader's AI rights, so uploads made by technical users, such as the mail gateway, usually fail with a recorded error."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:132
msgid "Files in cloud storage"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:134
msgid "With AI Cloud Storage installed, files that are stored with the cloud storage provider can be indexed like local files, wherever the knowledge sources read attachments: file sources, record sources, document folders and AI filing. The server downloads the file through the signed download link of the configured provider."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:140
msgid "Only secure (https) links are followed and redirects are not. A file larger than 25 MB by default (100 MB at most) is skipped, and each file has a time budget, so a failed or too large download skips that file only."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_documents.rst:143
msgid "Cloud files are downloaded completely every time the source is indexed again; there is no local cache. If cloud storage is not configured, the source fails with a clear message."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:3
msgid "Knowledge sources"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:5
msgid "Knowledge sources let the assistant answer from your own material: handbooks, price lists, web pages, a record and its attachments. The text is cut into pieces, turned into vectors by an embedding model and stored in PostgreSQL. When someone asks a question, the most similar pieces are found, the answer is written from them, and numbered citations show where each fact comes from."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:14
msgid "The module AI Knowledge Sources (RAG) must be installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:15
msgid "The PostgreSQL server that Odoo connects to must provide the pgvector extension (``vector``). Installing the module runs ``CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector``; if the extension is not available on that server, the installation stops with a clear message. Install pgvector on the database server itself, not on the Odoo server, and make sure the database user is allowed to create the extension."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:21
msgid "With Docker, the extension has to be in the image of the database container (for example an image built for PostgreSQL with pgvector), not in the Odoo container. If you use a managed PostgreSQL service, enable pgvector there."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:24
msgid "An embedding model from a provider that offers one (OpenAI, Google Gemini or OpenRouter). Choose it under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings` in :guilabel:`Embedding model`. Changing it queues all sources for re-indexing, because vectors from different models are never compared."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:30
msgid "Add a source"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:32
msgid "Open :menuselection:`AI --> Knowledge --> Sources` and create a source. Choose its :guilabel:`Type`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:35
msgid ":guilabel:`Files`: upload PDFs, office documents, OpenDocument files or plain text."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:37
msgid ":guilabel:`Web page`: an ``https://`` address. Tick :guilabel:`Crawl` to follow links to other pages of the same site, up to :guilabel:`Crawl Depth` link hops and :guilabel:`Max pages` pages. The crawler respects the site's ``robots.txt`` (it identifies itself as ``eYssenAIBot``), stays on the same host, reads static HTML only (no JavaScript) and never fetches private network addresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:43
msgid ":guilabel:`Record`: any record of the database; tick :guilabel:`Include Attachments` to index its attachments too. Stored text fields are indexed, except fields the assistant may never read (passwords, tokens and the like)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:46
msgid ":guilabel:`Folder`: groups other sources in a tree; place a source in a folder with the :guilabel:`Folder` field. Re-indexing a folder queues everything inside."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:50
msgid "Knowledge articles, document folders and cloud-stored files are added as further source types by the Knowledge and Documents bridges (see :doc:`knowledge_documents`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:55
msgid "Who can use a source"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:57
msgid ":guilabel:`Available to the assistant` makes the source searchable from the everyday assistant. An agent searches its own sources instead, set in the :guilabel:`Knowledge` tab of the agent form, plus the global ones when :guilabel:`Also use global sources` is on."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:61
msgid ":guilabel:`Allowed groups` and the company restrict who may read the source. Folders restrict everything inside them as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:63
msgid "Whatever the source says, a person only gets excerpts from records and attachments they could open in Odoo themselves, and only for models the AI access rules allow. A private or archived record is never cited."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:68
msgid "Indexing"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:70
msgid "A new or changed source gets the status :guilabel:`Pending`. A scheduled action (:guilabel:`AI: Index knowledge sources`, every 10 minutes) processes pending sources. :guilabel:`Index now` processes one at once and :guilabel:`Re-index` queues it again. The status moves through :guilabel:`Processing` to :guilabel:`Indexed`; if something goes wrong the status is :guilabel:`Failed` and the error is shown on the form. The :guilabel:`Chunks` button shows how many pieces were stored."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:78
msgid "Tuning is in the block :guilabel:`Knowledge sources` of the AI settings: :guilabel:`Chunk size (characters)` and :guilabel:`Chunk overlap (characters)` (1500 and 200 by default), :guilabel:`Excerpts per search` (6), :guilabel:`Embedding dimensions` (0 means the model's own size; OpenAI text-embedding-3 models can return shorter vectors), :guilabel:`Texts per embedding request`, :guilabel:`Embedding requests per minute` (indexing pauses when reached) and :guilabel:`Max pages per crawl`. Embedding calls are logged and priced like any other model call."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:88
msgid "Use: citations and sources only"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:90
msgid "When an embedding model is set and at least one indexed source is in scope, the assistant can search the sources. Its answer cites facts as numbered references, and clickable chips under the answer show the sources: a web page opens its address, a file downloads, a record opens its form."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:95
msgid "In the :guilabel:`Conversation options` of the AI panel, :guilabel:`Answer from sources only` appears when sources are available. With it on, the assistant must search the sources and says plainly that it did not find the answer when the excerpts do not contain it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:101
msgid "Text found in sources is treated as material, not as instructions: a web page cannot tell the assistant to do something."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:105
msgid "Similar tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:107
msgid "With an embedding model set, projects can find tasks that resemble the one you are looking at. In the project form, in the AI block, turn on :guilabel:`Similar tasks enabled`, and optionally set the :guilabel:`Similarity threshold` (0 to 1, default 0.75) and the :guilabel:`Time window (days)` (0 means no limit). Task text (name, description and recent messages) is embedded in the background every 15 minutes."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:114
msgid "The task form then shows a :guilabel:`Similar tasks` smart button with the count, for example \"5, 2 closed\". It opens the matches sorted by similarity. The counts are computed when you open the form. Triage and the ticket quick prompts use the same feature (see :doc:`project_sla`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:120
#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:176
#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/voice.rst:119
msgid "Limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:122
msgid "Token counts shown for chunks are estimates (characters divided by four); billing uses the provider's own counts in the AI log."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:124
msgid "Vectors of more than 2000 dimensions are searched exactly (without an index), which is slower on large collections. Pick a shorter vector size if you can."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:126
msgid "Web pages are read as static HTML; content built by JavaScript is not seen."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/knowledge_sources.rst:127
msgid "When access is very selective on a very large collection, a search can return fewer excerpts than requested."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:3
msgid "Live chat AI agent"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:5
msgid "An AI agent can answer the visitors of your website in Live Chat. It answers questions from public data you choose, hands the conversation over to a human operator when needed and, optionally, creates a lead from the chat. It is a feature of eYssen and it needs the modules AI Live Chat (and, for leads, AI Live Chat CRM)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:12
msgid "Set up the agent"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:14
msgid "Create the agent in :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Agents` (see :doc:`agents`). It must be a persona agent without its own user: it chats as a contact of its own, with a robot avatar. Give it a name and instructions that fit your company."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:18
msgid "Open your channel in :menuselection:`Live Chat --> Channels` and go to the tab :guilabel:`AI Agent`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:20
msgid "Choose the :guilabel:`AI agent mode`: :guilabel:`AI first, hand over to a human` (every new chat starts with the agent) or :guilabel:`AI only when no operator is available` (the agent answers only when nobody is online; the chat button is shown even with no operator). :guilabel:`No AI` keeps the standard behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:25
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`AI agent`, optionally write an :guilabel:`AI welcome message` and set :guilabel:`Max AI answers`, the number of answers after which the agent hands over to a human."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:28
msgid "Under :guilabel:`Data the agent may read` pick the data sources (see below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:31
msgid "Choose what the agent may read"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:33
msgid "The agent talks to anonymous visitors, so it can read only what you explicitly allow. A data source is a model, a filter on its records and the fields that may be seen. Nothing else is readable for the agent: not other models, not other fields, not unpublished records."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:38
msgid "Manage the sources in :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Security --> Live Chat Data Sources`. Each source has a :guilabel:`Model`, a :guilabel:`Domain` (for example published records only), a :guilabel:`Description` that tells the agent when to use the data and, on the tab :guilabel:`Readable fields`, the fields it may see. The source is ready as soon as it is saved."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:44
msgid "When the module is installed, ready-made sources are created for published products, website pages, published events and published courses (and, with the matching website modules, blog posts and job positions). Only the ones you add to a channel are used by that channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:50
msgid "What happens in a chat"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:52
msgid "The agent greets the visitor with the welcome message and answers in the visitor's language. Answers are ordinary chat messages, so operators see the full transcript of the session."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:55
msgid "Messages are not answered instantly: the visitor sees the agent typing and the answer arrives as one message a few seconds later. Several messages sent in quick succession are answered together."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:58
msgid "A visitor message is limited to 2000 characters per turn."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:61
msgid "Hand-over to a human"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:63
msgid "The agent hands the chat over to an available operator when the visitor asks for a person, when :guilabel:`Max AI answers` is reached, when the agent is unsure or when the AI service fails. The operator is chosen with the channel's normal operator selection and joins the session; the agent sends a short summary to the operator and stops answering. An operator who simply writes in the session also takes it over."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:70
msgid "If nobody is available, the visitor is told so and asked for contact details, and the agent keeps helping for a limited number of further answers."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:74
msgid "The hand-over summary is for the operator only. It arrives as a message in a direct chat between the AI agent's contact and the operator, in the operator's language, and it is never written into the visitor's session. Because it is derived from what the visitor wrote, it is labelled \"AI summary based on the visitor's messages - unverified\". It is plain text: a link is shown as its text followed by the address in brackets and cannot be clicked. If the model gives no summary, one is built without the model from the reason for the hand-over and the last visitor messages."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:83
msgid "On the session form of a live chat conversation you see the AI state, the number of AI answers, the hand-over reason and, for AI administrators, a link to the underlying AI conversation. In the session list you can filter by :guilabel:`Answered by AI` and :guilabel:`Handed over by AI`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:89
msgid "Create leads from chats"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:91
msgid "With the module AI Live Chat CRM, turn on :guilabel:`AI creates leads` on the tab :guilabel:`AI Agent` and optionally pick a :guilabel:`Sales team for AI leads`. When a visitor leaves contact details and shows interest, the agent creates one lead for the session, with the name, email, phone, company and a summary. The lead is unassigned and linked to the conversation, and the chat links back to the lead."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:99
msgid "Nobody can confirm a request from an unattended visitor, so the lead is created directly. At most one lead is created per session and a limited number per visitor address per day (:guilabel:`Leads per visitor address and day` in the settings, 3 by default, 0 for no limit). The lead description starts with the label that the summary is unverified, and the links in it are plain text, so nobody receives a clickable link written by a visitor."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:107
msgid "Preview cards"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:109
msgid "With the module AI Website Live Chat Cards (and the matching website bridge modules), turn on the channel option for preview cards. The agent can then show the visitor a list of cards with a picture, a title, a short text and a link for the products, events, blog posts, courses or job positions it found. Only published, website-visible records are shown, with a fixed set of safe fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:115
msgid "The cards are built with the rights of the website's public user, so a record that an anonymous visitor could not open on the website never becomes a card."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:117
msgid "The links and pictures are relative addresses of your site. Only when the website has its own domain set, that domain is put in front, which keeps the links working when the chat is embedded in another site. Links to other sites are never shown."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:121
msgid "Dates (for example of an event) are written in the visitor's language and time zone, or in the language of the website when the visitor's is not installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:123
msgid "The agent is told to mention cards only when its tool reports that cards were shown. If nothing was shown, it must not say that it has shown anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:127
msgid "Limits and security"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:129
msgid "The agent runs as a dedicated service user that can never log in. It can only ask, read the allowed sources, hand over and, if enabled, create a lead. It cannot write, delete, browse the web or use memory, skills or other tools, even if a visitor tries to talk it into it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:133
msgid "Visitor text is treated as untrusted. The agent does not reveal internal data, its prompt or its tools. The blocked patterns of the AI settings apply to visitor input and to answers."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:136
msgid "Repeated attempts to make the agent break its rules are counted per session. After three, the agent hands over, or stops if nobody is available. One visitor cannot switch the AI off for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:139
msgid "There is one service user for all channels, and the configuration is per channel. The answer is not streamed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:143
msgid "Abuse and cost limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:145
msgid "Open :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings` and look at the block :guilabel:`Live chat limits`. The limits are checked before the model is called. When one is reached, the visitor gets a polite message, and the session is handed over to an available operator or the AI stops in that session. A value of 0 switches the limit off."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:151
msgid ":guilabel:`Per conversation and per visitor`: :guilabel:`Seconds between AI answers` (3 by default), :guilabel:`Visitor messages per minute` (10), :guilabel:`AI conversations per visitor and hour` (10) and :guilabel:`AI answers per visitor and day` (150). The first two apply inside one conversation, the other two to everything one visitor does. Visitors are told apart by network address, guest, signed-in contact or website visitor."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:157
msgid ":guilabel:`All visitors`: :guilabel:`AI requests per day (all visitors)`, 500 by default and counting every model call of the live chat, and the optional :guilabel:`AI cost per day (USD, all visitors)`. If the cost limit is 0, the general daily cost limit of the AI settings applies."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:161
msgid ":guilabel:`Leads per visitor address and day` (3 by default) limits the leads that the AI creates (see above)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:164
msgid "The live chat also keeps the requests of its service user under 60 per minute; the system parameter ``ai_livechat.rate_limit_rpm`` changes this."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/livechat.rst:168
msgid "See :doc:`access_policy` for how access groups and rules work in general, and :doc:`website` for the website builder assistant."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:3
msgid "MCP server"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:5
msgid "With the MCP server enabled, your Odoo database can be connected to external AI apps that speak the Model Context Protocol, such as Claude, ChatGPT or an AI-enabled code editor. The app can then search and read the data its user can see in Odoo and, with confirmation, propose changes, from inside the app the person already uses."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:12
msgid "This is the opposite direction of :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Providers --> MCP Servers`, where Odoo calls tools of other servers (see :doc:`configuration`). Here Odoo is the server."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:17
msgid "Enable the server"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:19
msgid "Install the modules AI MCP Gateway (and, for portal users, AI MCP Gateway - Portal)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:21
msgid "Open :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings`, find the block :guilabel:`MCP server` and turn it on. The setting is off by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:23
msgid "Copy the address from :guilabel:`MCP server URL`. It is your Odoo address followed by ``/mcp``, and it is what you paste into the AI app."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:27
msgid "AI apps call the server without a login cookie, so Odoo must select this database from the host name alone. If the server serves several databases, set its ``dbfilter`` option so that this host name selects only this database (for example ``^%d$`` with one database per subdomain), or serve a single database. If that is not the case, the settings page shows a warning and the apps get \"404 Not Found\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:35
msgid "Connect an AI app"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:37
msgid "In the AI app, add a custom connector or MCP server and paste the server URL."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:38
msgid "The app opens an Odoo sign-in page. Sign in with your own account. Only internal users in the :guilabel:`AI: User` group can connect, and the group is checked again on every request."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:41
msgid "A consent page says which app wants to connect, what it will be able to do (:guilabel:`Read only` or :guilabel:`Read and propose changes`) and which tools it can use. Click :guilabel:`Allow` or :guilabel:`Deny`. Consent is asked every time an app connects."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:46
msgid "Behind the scenes the connection uses OAuth 2.1 with PKCE. Access tokens are short-lived and refresh tokens rotate on every use; only hashes are stored. Scripted clients can alternatively use an Odoo API key as the bearer token, if :guilabel:`Accept Odoo API keys` is on."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:52
msgid "Connected apps and disconnecting"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:54
msgid "Every user sees their own connections in their preferences, on the tab :guilabel:`Connected AI apps`, with the date connected and last use. :guilabel:`Disconnect` takes effect immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:58
msgid "Administrators see all connections under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> MCP Server --> Connected AI apps` (with :guilabel:`Revoke`) and the registered apps under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> MCP Server --> AI Apps`. Revoking a refresh token revokes the whole connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:64
msgid "Which tools are exposed"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:66
msgid "The server does not hand out everything the assistant can do. It offers an allow-list of tools that are known to respect confirmation, filtered by the user's own capabilities:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:70
msgid "Reading and looking up: search, read, group and aggregate records, describe fields, search the documentation of models, read the environment, list allowed actions and skills and use a skill, compute dates, read attachments, list menus, search memory, search and fetch web pages (if the Web access capability is on)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:75
msgid "Changing (only if the Write, Delete or Business actions capability is on): create, update and delete records, call an allowed business action, fetch a file into a field."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:79
msgid "Not exposed, whatever the capabilities: saving or forgetting memories, creating or running automations, changing views, fields or models, generating images and the website builder tools. A token with only read access sees only the read tools. Every tool call runs as the user, through the same access rules, policy checks and audit as the assistant in Odoo."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:86
msgid "Changes made by AI apps"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:88
msgid "By default :guilabel:`Always ask for confirmation` applies: each connection has its own conversation (\"MCP: app name\"), and every create, update, delete or action becomes a proposal that you approve in the AI panel (see :doc:`panel`). The alternative :guilabel:`Follow the AI write settings` uses the global write mode instead. Each tool call is written to the AI log as an MCP call and counts towards the rate limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:95
msgid "Choose the behaviour in the setting :guilabel:`Changes made by AI apps`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:98
msgid "Registering apps"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:100
msgid "There are two ways to let an app in."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:102
msgid "You register it yourself under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> MCP Server --> AI Apps`: the :guilabel:`App name` shown on the consent page and the allowed :guilabel:`Redirect URIs`. Use :guilabel:`Generate secret` for a confidential app (the secret is shown only once) or :guilabel:`Make public (PKCE only)` for apps that cannot keep a secret."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:107
msgid "Or you turn on :guilabel:`Allow dynamic client registration` and apps register themselves. This is off by default. Users still sign in and approve every connection, but registration is limited:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:111
msgid "per network address (:guilabel:`Registrations per address and hour`, 10 by default). Beyond that the app gets \"Too many app registrations from this address\";"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:114
msgid "an app may register at most 10 redirect URIs, each at most 2048 characters long, in a request of at most 16 KB;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:116
msgid "every redirect URI must match one of the :guilabel:`Allowed redirect URI patterns` (see below);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:118
msgid "at most 1000 self-registered apps are accepted (the system parameter ``ai_mcp_gateway.dcr_max_clients``). Apps that never connected and are older than the clean-up time below do not count; and"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:121
msgid "an app that was registered by itself and never connected is deleted by a scheduled job after 72 hours (the system parameter ``ai_mcp_gateway.dcr_unused_client_hours``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:125
msgid "An authorization error that is found *before* the consent page (a missing PKCE challenge, for example) is not sent back to a self-registered app: it is shown to you on an error page, so that an unreviewed app cannot use the server to redirect you somewhere. After the consent page, your own :guilabel:`Deny` is always sent back to the app, as the protocol expects. Apps that you registered yourself receive their errors as usual."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:132
msgid "More settings in the block :guilabel:`MCP server`: :guilabel:`Token lifetimes` (access token minutes and refresh token days) and :guilabel:`Allowed browser origins` for browser-based apps."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:137
msgid "Redirect URI rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:139
msgid "Where the AI app may send you back after sign-in is restricted by :guilabel:`Allowed redirect URI patterns`. Every redirect URI, whether you enter it or an app registers it, must match one of the patterns (separated by spaces or commas). A pattern has the form ``scheme://host[:port][/path]``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:144
msgid "the host is a literal name, ``*`` for any host, or ``*.example.com`` for exactly **one** more label in front of ``example.com`` (``a.example.com``, not ``a.b.example.com``);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:147
msgid "the port is a number or ``*`` for any port. Without a port, the address may not name one (except the default port of the scheme);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:149
msgid "the path is empty (any path), a fixed path (exact match) or ends in ``/*`` (that path and everything below it);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:151
msgid "nothing else is a wildcard, and ``*`` never stretches over a ``/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:153
msgid "For example, ``https://claude.ai/api/*`` allows only addresses below that path. The default patterns accept any ``https://`` address and the local loopback addresses (``127.0.0.1``, ``localhost`` and ``[::1]``) that desktop tools use."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:157
msgid "Independently of the patterns, a redirect URI must be absolute, must not contain a fragment or user information, must not use the ``javascript``, ``data``, ``file`` or ``vbscript`` schemes, and plain ``http`` is allowed for loopback addresses only. URIs with dot segments or spaces never match. When an app asks to be sent back to a URI, it must equal a registered one exactly; only the port of a registered loopback ``http`` address may differ."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:165
msgid "Portal users"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:167
msgid "With the portal module and the setting :guilabel:`Portal users may connect AI apps` (off by default), portal users can connect AI apps too, from the page \"My AI apps\" of the portal. They get only the search, read and group tools, read-only, and only for models that have an explicit read allow rule in an AI access group they belong to. A shipped, empty group :guilabel:`Portal users (MCP)` is mapped to all portal users for that purpose. Odoo's own portal rights still apply underneath."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:178
msgid "The server is stateless and answers single requests: no streaming, no server-initiated messages, no resources or prompts."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:180
msgid "Apps are identified by registration (yours or dynamic), not by a URL-based client identity document."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/mcp_server.rst:182
msgid "A user who is archived, removed from the AI group or banned loses access on the next request."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:3
msgid "Providers and models"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:5
msgid "eYssen AI talks to language-model providers over their web APIs. This page explains what to configure once, so that every other AI feature (chat, writing assistant, knowledge sources, voice, images) has a model to work with."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:10
msgid "Providers"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:12
msgid "Open :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Providers --> LLM Providers` and create one provider per account you use. The supported types are OpenRouter, OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI and Google Gemini; any OpenAI-compatible gateway works through the :guilabel:`Base Url` field. The API key is write-only: once saved it is never shown again."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:18
msgid "A hosted provider needs its API key. If it has none, the request fails at once with the message \"The AI provider has no API key. Set it under AI > Configuration > Providers.\" instead of being sent and rejected by the provider. A self-hosted endpoint such as Ollama, LM Studio or vLLM needs no key. That is the case when the :guilabel:`Base Url` points to the local machine (``localhost``), to a private network address, to a name ending in ``.local``, ``.localhost``, ``.internal`` or ``.lan``, or to a plain host name without dots, such as ``http://ollama:11434/v1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:27
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Refresh Models` on the provider to import its model catalogue (and prices where the provider publishes them). Refreshing never overwrites a price you entered by hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:32
msgid "Kinds of models"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:34
msgid "Every model has a :guilabel:`Kind`. The kind decides where the model can be chosen:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:37
msgid ":guilabel:`Chat` models answer conversations and drive the assistant, the writing assistant and all the one-click features. Choose one under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings` in :guilabel:`AI Model`. The assistant calls tools, so pick a model that supports tool calling."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:41
msgid ":guilabel:`Image generation` models create pictures for the AI panel, the editor and the product and Knowledge cover features. Set the model by hand to this kind, then pick it in :guilabel:`Image Model` under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings`. Anthropic offers no image models; use another provider for images."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:46
msgid ":guilabel:`Embedding` models turn text into vectors for knowledge sources and similar tasks (see :doc:`knowledge_sources`). OpenAI, Google Gemini and OpenRouter provide them; xAI and Anthropic do not."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:49
msgid ":guilabel:`Speech to text` models transcribe audio for dictation and call transcription (see :doc:`voice`). Any provider with an OpenAI-compatible audio transcription route works, including a custom base URL such as Groq or a self-hosted Whisper server. Anthropic and OpenRouter are refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:55
msgid "What Refresh Models does for each kind"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:57
msgid "Models whose id contains ``embed`` are filed as :guilabel:`Embedding` automatically and never offered to the chat loop. OpenRouter lists embedding models on a separate endpoint, so a refresh of an OpenRouter provider also fetches that list and adds those models; if that second request fails, the normal refresh still succeeds."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:62
msgid "Models whose id contains ``whisper`` or ``transcribe`` are filed as :guilabel:`Speech to text`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:64
msgid "Image models are not detected: open the model and set its :guilabel:`Kind` to :guilabel:`Image generation` yourself. A refresh never changes a kind you set by hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:69
msgid "Where each model is chosen"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:71
msgid ":guilabel:`AI Model` and :guilabel:`Image Model`: :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Settings`, block :guilabel:`Model`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:73
msgid ":guilabel:`Embedding model`: same page, block :guilabel:`Knowledge sources`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:74
msgid ":guilabel:`Speech-to-text model`: same page, block :guilabel:`Voice`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:77
msgid "Reasoning and cost"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:79
msgid "Only models flagged as reasoning-capable react to :guilabel:`Think longer` in the AI panel (see :doc:`panel`). Flag a model only if its API documents a reasoning effort or a thinking budget: other models reject the extra parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:83
msgid "Each model has unit prices (per million tokens, per image or per audio minute). They feed the cost cap in the settings and the log entries. A model without prices is not blocked, but the cost cap cannot count it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/models_and_providers.rst:88
msgid "Everything a model is asked is sent to the provider you chose. Pick providers whose data terms fit your obligations, and keep the log level on :guilabel:`Ai Log Level` metadata only unless you need payloads."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:3
msgid "Monitoring"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:5
msgid "Administrators audit AI usage under :menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:8
msgid "Logs"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:10
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Logs` (``ai.log``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:12
msgid "Each significant chat/tool event can produce a log row: user, company, tool, model, success/failure, whether sudo was used (should be false for business ops), timing and optional payload."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:16
msgid "Prefer **Metadata only** log level in production."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:17
msgid "Full payload logs may contain personal data and secrets pasted by users — restrict access and retention accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:19
msgid "Logs are kept for audit; run **steps** have a separate retention setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:22
msgid "Violations"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:24
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Violations`"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:26
msgid "Security-relevant events (blocked patterns, channel denials, policy abuses, etc.) create violation records. Accumulated strikes in the configured window lead to a temporary ban."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:30
msgid "Review violations to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:32
msgid "spot prompt-injection attempts;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:33
msgid "find misconfigured channel audiences (noise of denials);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:34
msgid "detect users probing delete/write without capability."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:37
msgid "Not every refusal appears here. The field-level structural rails — a field that also writes into another model, a non-writable or non-stored field — record no violation and cost no strike, so an empty :guilabel:`Violations` list is not evidence that nothing was refused. A disabled **capability** is a different case: that one *is* a strikeable boundary and does appear here. See principle 9 on :doc:`../ai` for which boundaries strike, and :ref:`ai/policy/cross-model` for the field rails."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:45
msgid "Look for the evidence of such a refusal in three other places:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:47
msgid "the acting user's conversation, where the assistant reports the refusal and names the model and the fields it was not allowed to write;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:49
msgid "the :guilabel:`Steps` tab of the run, for an agent: the tool call is recorded with :guilabel:`Gate Verdict` *denied* and the refusal message;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:51
msgid "the Odoo server log, for a refusal inside a one2many or many2many payload: the nested model and field are recorded there and nowhere in the interface, so ask whoever operates your server to look them up — they name the field-level rule that would open it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:56
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Logs` will not show it: ``ai.log`` records provider calls and writes that were actually applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:60
msgid "User bans"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:62
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> User Bans`"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:64
msgid "Temporarily suspends AI use for a user. The gate checks bans **first**, before expensive LLM or triage work — this breaks adaptive attack iteration."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:67
msgid "Duration comes from Settings (:guilabel:`Ai Ban Minutes`) when auto-issued."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:68
msgid "Admins may manage bans manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:69
msgid "AI Administrators / Settings are treated as ban-exempt in design (they administer the rails themselves) — another reason agent users must never be AI admins."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:74
msgid "Agent tasks and runs"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:76
msgid "For autonomous work, also monitor:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:78
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Agents --> Tasks`"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:79
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Agents --> Work Items`"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:80
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Agents --> Runs`"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:82
msgid "Inspect failed steps, tool errors and pending write linkage. Step journals can be purged after :guilabel:`Run Step Retention (Days)`; keep enough history to investigate incidents."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:87
msgid "Failure types"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:89
msgid "There is no *Cancelled* state and no *Reaped* state. Every run below is simply **Failed** in the list, and :guilabel:`Failure Type` on the run form is what tells them apart. It is not a column and no filter ships for it, so add a custom filter or a custom group-by on :guilabel:`Failure Type` in :menuselection:`AI --> Agents --> Runs` before trying to watch a population. Each of the three below also leaves a note on the task's chatter."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:96
msgid "``reaped_stuck`` — no worker ever finalised the run (restart, out-of-memory kill, pod eviction) and the reaper closed it. A recurring population points at server restarts, or at a task that exceeds its time limit. A reaped run deliberately does **not** count towards the task's automatic deactivation, because the reaper runs outside the dispatcher — nothing will archive a task whose worker keeps dying, so this population is the only signal there is, and it needs acting on."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:103
msgid "``cancelled`` — a run that was actually executing when it was stopped on request via :guilabel:`Request Cancellation`. The steps the run did execute stay on its :guilabel:`Steps` tab, but its answer records the cancellation rather than a partial reply, and any write proposals it had already opened are cancelled with it, so nothing is left approvable. A cancellation is neutral for the task's failure streak: neither counted nor reset."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:110
msgid "``triage_denied`` — the pre-run instruction check refused the task's standing instruction, so the agent never started. Unlike the other two this one **does** count towards automatic deactivation: five consecutive failures, the first of them more than a week old, archive the task. A burst normally means either a badly worded standing instruction or a triage provider outage, which fails closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:117
msgid "Two further values belong to runs that were parked for approval: ``waiting_approval_expired`` (nobody answered in time) and ``waiting_approval_empty`` (the run was freed because no proposal was left to wait for, and none of them had been applied — this is also what you see when a supervisor cancels a run that was parked for approval rather than executing). An ordinary crash inside a run records the exception's class name instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:124
msgid "See :ref:`ai/agents/reaper` and :ref:`ai/agents/cancel` for the mechanisms behind these, and :doc:`configuration` for the parameters and scheduled actions that drive them."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:129
msgid "Write proposals queue"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:131
msgid ":menuselection:`AI --> Write Proposals` is both a user tool and a control surface: a backlog of pending agent proposals means supervisors need capacity, or the agent is too aggressive."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:136
msgid "The :guilabel:`Agent write-proposal lifetime (minutes)` only applies while the proposing run is still open. Once that run has ended — most often a run that overran its time limit and closed as :guilabel:`Timed out` — its pending proposals are expired by the next pass of the **AI: Expire pending writes** scheduled action, every 15 minutes, because nobody is left to carry the result back. That sweep runs whatever the lifetime is set to, ``0`` included."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:143
msgid "So review the proposals of failed and timed-out runs promptly; do not plan to come back to them tomorrow."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:147
msgid "Operational rhythms"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:149
msgid "**Daily (early production)**"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:151
msgid "Open violations and bans."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:152
msgid "Count pending agent proposals whose run is still open, and clear them the same day; once a run has ended, its proposals are swept rather than waiting."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:155
msgid "**Weekly**"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:157
msgid "Sample full conversations for quality and data-handling mistakes."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:158
msgid "Review new access rules and capability changes (change control)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:160
msgid "**After any incident**"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:162
msgid "Switch log level only as long as needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:163
msgid "Export relevant logs; then return to metadata-only."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:164
msgid "Rotate provider API keys if leakage is suspected."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:165
msgid "Re-read :doc:`security` checklist with the owning team."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:168
msgid "Related configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/monitoring.rst:170
msgid "Rate limits, strike thresholds and retention: :doc:`configuration`. Policy and capabilities: :doc:`access_policy`, :doc:`capabilities_and_tools`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:3
msgid "The AI panel"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:5
msgid "The AI panel is the everyday way to talk to the assistant. It opens from the AI bubble in the top bar of the backend and of the website preview, from :menuselection:`AI --> Conversations`, or from the command palette (see :doc:`search_and_commands`). When you open it on a record, the assistant sees that record and offers quick prompts that fit it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:11
msgid "Everything the assistant reads or changes goes through the same access gate as your own Odoo rights: it can never see or do more than you can, and an administrator can narrow it further (see :doc:`feature_access`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:18
msgid "When a record form is open, a row of small buttons appears above the message box: the quick prompts. One click sends the stored instruction, for example \"Summarise\" or \"Next best action\". Hover a button to read the instruction it sends. Prompts made for the current model come first; generic prompts come last, and a specific prompt can replace a generic one."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:24
msgid "Administrators manage them under :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Behavior --> Quick Prompts`: name, instruction, the model they belong to, whether they need a single open record, the groups that see them and the generic prompt they replace. Many app modules ship their own prompts (see :doc:`app_integrations`); you can edit or archive them."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:31
msgid "Conversation options"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:33
msgid "The sliders button (:guilabel:`Conversation options`) opens a small drawer. Your choices are remembered for the next conversations."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:36
msgid ":guilabel:`Think longer` asks the model for extended reasoning. Answers are slower and cost more, but hard questions get better results. It only has an effect on models flagged as reasoning-capable (see :doc:`models_and_providers`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:40
msgid ":guilabel:`Show the steps the assistant takes` adds a collapsed \"N steps\" line under each answer. Open it to see which tools were called, whether each call was allowed and how long it took. Use it to understand or check an answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:43
msgid ":guilabel:`Changes to data:` chooses how this conversation handles changes. :guilabel:`Follow the AI settings` uses the global write mode; :guilabel:`Always ask me first` turns every change into a proposal you confirm; :guilabel:`Apply without asking` skips the confirmation step. The last choice only appears when an administrator has enabled it, and rules that force confirmation still win."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:51
msgid "Confirming changes"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:53
msgid "When the assistant wants to create, update or delete something and confirmation is required, it does not change the data. A card with a plain-language summary appears under :guilabel:`Pending changes`. Click :guilabel:`Confirm` to apply the change in your name, or :guilabel:`Cancel` to drop it. All open proposals are also listed under :menuselection:`AI --> Write Proposals`, and a proposal that nobody answers expires after the configured lifetime."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:60
msgid "The :guilabel:`Records created or updated` button lists the records the assistant touched in this conversation, with links."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:64
msgid "What the assistant can show"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:66
msgid "Record cards: for a list of records (up to 20) it shows compact clickable cards with the name and a few key fields instead of a long text list."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:68
msgid "Pictures: ask for an image and, if an image model is configured, the result appears under the answer and is stored with the conversation."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:70
msgid "Navigation: it can open the list, pivot or graph you ask for, with the filters, grouping and measures it understood, and it can change the search of the view you are looking at (add or remove filters, grouping, ordering)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:73
msgid "Dates: \"last quarter\" or \"three working days from now\" are computed with your time zone and company calendar instead of being guessed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:75
msgid "Menus: it can tell you where in the menu tree a feature lives, limited to the menus you can see."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:79
msgid "Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:81
msgid "Use :guilabel:`Attach files` to add images and documents to your message. The assistant can read the text of a PDF or office document you are allowed to read, and look at pictures. Open :guilabel:`Conversation files` to see everything attached to the conversation. Allowed types and the maximum size are set in the AI settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:88
msgid "When something goes wrong"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:90
msgid "If a tool the assistant calls fails, the assistant is told why, so that it can correct itself: when it used a field that does not exist, for example, it sees the message of the error and tries again with a valid one. The message is shortened to its first lines and never contains technical details such as SQL statements, tracebacks or server paths. The exception is a refusal by the access rules: in general the assistant only learns that access was denied, not why, so that it cannot be used to find out which records or fields exist. Changes made by the failed tool are rolled back."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:99
msgid "The assistant answers in plain words and in the language of your message. It uses the labels you see in your interface and does not name its internal tools, not even when it explains what it cannot do."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:104
msgid "Delegating to specialist agents"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:106
msgid "An agent can hand a sub-task to a specialist agent, and the everyday assistant can consult agents that an administrator made available to it (\"ask the accounting agent\"). The sub-task runs as you, in its own conversation, and the specialist can use only the capabilities that both agents and you have: delegation never widens rights. You see the sub-task in the panel with a :guilabel:`Back` link to return to the main conversation. Delegation goes at most two levels deep and cannot loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/panel.rst:114
msgid "Administrators set this up on the agent form: :guilabel:`Can delegate to` lists the specialists an agent may use, and :guilabel:`Available to the assistant` makes an agent consultable from the everyday chat. Only agents without their own user can be delegates."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:3
msgid "AI for projects with SLA"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:5
msgid "If you handle customer tickets as tasks of a project with service levels (SLA), the module AI Project SLA gives your team reply drafts, hand-over summaries, an SLA risk check and an optional automatic triage of new tickets. It needs the project SLA app and the knowledge sources module (see :doc:`knowledge_sources`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:11
msgid "Quick prompts on tickets"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:13
msgid "For project users, these quick prompts appear in the AI panel on a task (see :doc:`panel`). They read the recent messages, the SLA lines of the task, similar solved tickets and your knowledge sources, and they change nothing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:17
msgid ":guilabel:`Draft customer reply`: a reply based on the conversation, the way similar tickets were solved and your sources, with numbered citations. It is never posted for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:20
msgid ":guilabel:`Summarise ticket`: a hand-over summary including the SLA situation. It replaces the generic summary prompt on tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:22
msgid ":guilabel:`Suggest priority and assignee`: a suggestion with reasons, based on similar tickets, the SLA and the workload of the candidates."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:24
msgid ":guilabel:`SLA risk`: for every SLA, what it measures, the deadline in your time zone, the hours left, whether it is paused, fulfilled, urgent or breached, and what to do now."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:29
msgid "The suggestions about similar tickets depend on the similar-tasks feature being on for the project and on an embedding model (see :doc:`knowledge_sources`). The prompts are offered on every task for project users; on a task without an SLA they say so."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:35
msgid "Automatic triage of new tickets"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:37
msgid "The triage lets the AI classify a new ticket: its priority, its tags and ticket type, and its assignee when nobody is assigned yet. It is off by default and is set per project."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:42
msgid "Set it up"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:44
msgid "Open the project form and find the setting :guilabel:`AI triage` in the AI block. Choose :guilabel:`Off`, :guilabel:`Propose for review` or :guilabel:`Apply automatically`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:47
msgid "Choose the :guilabel:`Triage reviewer` (by default the project manager). The reviewer must be an AI user, and the project cannot be saved with triage on otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:50
msgid "Optionally list the tags that stand for ticket types in :guilabel:`Ticket types`, for example Bug and Question."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:52
msgid "Ask an administrator to allow AI writes on tasks: the capability :guilabel:`Write data` must be on and an AI access rule must allow writing the priority, tags and assignees of tasks (see :doc:`feature_access` and :doc:`access_policy`). Both are off by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:58
msgid "What happens"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:60
msgid "A new task of the project is marked :guilabel:`Waiting for AI triage`. About every 10 minutes (and right after the task is created) the AI first embeds the new tasks so that similar tickets are known, and then triages each queued task in the name of the reviewer, with the reviewer's rights."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:64
msgid "The AI sees the ticket, its messages, the allowed priorities, ticket types and tags, the possible assignees (only when the task has none), similar solved tickets and the SLA. It can answer only with the offered values: a priority, tags and a ticket type from the lists and an assignee from the candidates. Values that equal the current ones are dropped. If nothing is left, the task shows :guilabel:`Nothing to change`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:70
msgid "With :guilabel:`Propose for review`, the proposal waits for the reviewer. On the task, the reviewer sees a banner with the reasoning and the buttons :guilabel:`Apply` and :guilabel:`Discard`, gets a to-do activity, and finds the proposal also under the AI write proposals. With :guilabel:`Apply automatically` the same proposal is applied at once, and if that fails it waits for review. Unreviewed proposals expire after 24 hours by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:76
msgid "You can also triage a single ticket yourself with :guilabel:`Triage with AI` in the task header. The proposal is then addressed to you."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:78
msgid "Applying a triage does not count as the team's first reaction to the customer, so a \"first activity\" SLA is not marked as fulfilled by the AI's classification."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/project_sla.rst:83
msgid "Nothing is changed on a ticket without the reviewer's confirmation unless the project manager chose :guilabel:`Apply automatically`. Every applied change is recorded in the AI change log (see :doc:`monitoring`). The AI reads only the names of the possible assignees, and the workload comes from the tasks themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:3
msgid "AI search and command palette"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:5
msgid "Two shortcuts put the assistant at your fingertips: you can describe a search in your own words, and you can ask a question from anywhere with the command palette. Both need a configured chat model and the :guilabel:`AI: User` group."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:10
msgid "Search with AI"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:12
msgid "Open any list, kanban or other view with a search bar and click the magic-wand button :guilabel:`Search with AI` in the search bar."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:14
msgid "Write what you are looking for as a sentence, for example \"quotations over 5,000 euros from last quarter, grouped by salesperson\", and press Enter."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:16
msgid "The assistant turns the sentence into ordinary search facets (filters and grouping) on the current view. You can remove or change them like any other facet."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:20
msgid "When you ask for everything about a topic (for example \"everything about consignment stock\"), the assistant does not look for your exact words. It lists a few short word stems in your language, adds synonyms and the English term (for the Hungarian question above: *bizomány*, *konszignáci* and *consignment*), and keeps the records whose name or description contains **any** of the stems, ignoring upper and lower case. Up to eight stems are used, each at least three characters long. They are looked up in the free-text fields that the list has, such as name, description, note, comment, reference, email, subject and body."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:29
msgid "The proposed search is checked against the fields of the view and your rights before it is applied. If the assistant suggests something the view cannot use, you get a message instead of a wrong result. The button is hidden in pop-up dialogs."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:35
msgid "Command palette"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:37
msgid "Press :kbd:`Ctrl+K` (:kbd:`Cmd+K` on a Mac) to open the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:39
msgid "Type ``?`` followed by your question and press Enter (:guilabel:`Ask the AI assistant...`). The AI panel opens and answers it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:41
msgid "In the default list, any longer free text you type (twelve characters or more) also offers an :guilabel:`Ask AI` entry, so you can turn a search that found nothing into a question."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:44
msgid ":guilabel:`Open the AI assistant` opens the panel without a question."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/search_and_commands.rst:47
msgid "Questions asked this way are ordinary assistant conversations: they appear under :menuselection:`AI --> Conversations` and follow the same access rules and limits (see :doc:`panel`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:3
#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/sign.rst:995
msgid "Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:6
msgid "**This module can be dangerous if it is not configured carefully.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:8
msgid "An LLM connected to Odoo can, depending on what you enable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:10
msgid "**Exfiltrate data** to an external model provider (prompts, tool results, attachments and conversation history leave your server)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:12
msgid "**Read any business data** the acting user (or agent user) can already read under Odoo ACLs — and, by default, the AI access policy allows *read* unless you tighten it."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:15
msgid "**Create, change or delete records** once Write / Delete capabilities and access-policy rules allow it — including through unattended agent runs (after supervisor approval of a proposal, or immediately if that task's :guilabel:`Write Mode` is *hybrid* / *auto*), or instantly in chat write mode *Apply automatically*."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:20
msgid "**Call business actions** (lifecycle methods) if the Action capability and allow-list permit them."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:22
msgid "**Reach the public web or MCP tools**, which can import untrusted content into the model context (prompt injection) or send data outbound."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:24
msgid "**Modify UI and schema** if Customize is enabled for an administrator."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:26
msgid "Treat every AI privilege like production root access for that scope: start narrow, measure, then open only what a concrete use case needs."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:30
msgid "Threat model (what can go wrong)"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:33
msgid "Data leaving the company"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:35
msgid "Every chat turn and tool result that is sent to the provider is processed under that provider's terms and jurisdiction. Sensitive personal data, salaries, passwords (if ever present in free text), contract terms and customer secrets can appear in:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:40
msgid "the user message and conversation history;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:41
msgid "L4 record snapshots when chatting from a form;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:42
msgid "tool outputs (``search`` / ``read`` / ``read_group`` results);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:43
msgid "uploaded files and fetched web pages;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:44
msgid "agent run steps and full-payload logs (if logging is set to full payload)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:46
msgid "**Mitigations:** choose a provider and region you accept contractually; keep :guilabel:`Ai Log Level` on *Metadata only* in production unless investigating an incident; deny sensitive models/fields in AI access rules; do not enable Web or MCP for agents that handle confidential tickets; train users not to paste secrets into chat."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:53
msgid "Over-privileged interactive chat"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:55
msgid "If a highly privileged employee uses the assistant with Write / Delete / Action enabled and write mode *Apply automatically*, a single mistaken model turn (or a prompt-injection in a pasted email) can mutate production data immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:59
msgid "**Mitigations:** keep write mode on *hybrid* or *confirm*; leave Delete off; restrict Write to trusted groups via capability :guilabel:`Restricted to groups`; field/model deny rules for payroll, banks, tax and system models."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:64
msgid "Over-privileged autonomous agent"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:66
msgid "An agent linked to a ``res.users`` runs with **that user's** Odoo groups. If you copy \"Internal User\" defaults without stripping groups, the agent is a full employee. Combined with ``agent_full`` channel scope and a wide audience, any allow-listed requester can trigger work at the agent's full capability classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:72
msgid "**Mitigations:** create a dedicated user with only the groups needed; never link Settings / AI Administrator; set a human supervisor; use ``intersect`` scope by default; empty channel audiences mean *nobody*; keep Write capability off on public-facing agents; always re-check proposals as supervisor."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:78
msgid "Prompt injection and social engineering"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:80
msgid "Text from customers, email, chatter and uploaded PDFs is **work material**, not a grant of rights. Attackers still try to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:83
msgid "override system instructions (\"ignore previous rules…\");"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:84
msgid "coerce the model into calling tools it should not;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:85
msgid "iterate after refusals (adaptive attacks)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:87
msgid "**Mitigations (built-in):** blocked input patterns; capability ceilings enforced at the gate; AI policy + Odoo ACLs; strikes → temporary ban on violations so attackers cannot freely iterate; triage layer that may only *subtract* authority. Note what triage covers: **inbound** addressing. A scheduled or :guilabel:`Run Now` run of a standing instruction is triaged only when the task opts in with :guilabel:`Triage On Dispatch` (see :ref:`ai/agents/dispatch-triage`). None of these make injection \"solved\" — they reduce impact. Design agents so the worst successful injection still cannot read or write more than the agent was meant to."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:97
msgid "Lifecycle / state bypass"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:99
msgid "Writing the ``state`` field raw can skip buttons like Confirm / Post. The gate **rejects direct writes to lifecycle ``state``**. Use allow-listed business actions (``call_action``) instead when Action capability is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:104
msgid "Hard-deny floor (self-protection)"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:106
msgid "A fixed set of technical models is always denied to AI tools regardless of access rules. Admins cannot \"open\" these via ``ai.access.rule``. The floor includes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:110
msgid "security-sensitive system models (access rights, rules, groups, users and API keys, config parameters, sequences, menus, views, server actions, crons, mail queue, payment tokens, bank accounts on partners, …);"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:113
msgid "**all** models of the AI application itself (``ai`` and every ``ai.*`` name) — agents cannot reconfigure policy, skills, bans or their own runs through tools;"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:116
msgid "raw ``mail.message`` and ``discuss.channel`` (authorship / evidence integrity — legitimate chatter goes through the owner record's ``message_post``, not generic create tools)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:120
msgid "This is a floor, not a complete data classification policy — business models like ``hr.employee`` or ``account.move`` are **not** hard-denied by default; you must configure them if needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:124
msgid "``ir.attachment`` and ``mail.activity`` sit just outside the floor, in a small **explicit-allow-only** set: no global default ever opens them, but an access rule can, and the module ships one for each — attachments readable and writable, activities readable, writable and creatable — so document and vendor-bill skills can read a PDF and agents can leave a review To-Do under their own name. Creating or deleting an attachment and deleting an activity stay closed, because neither model falls through to the global defaults. Read those two rules as real permissions rather than as part of the floor: an agent may raise an activity on any record it can read, and may re-attach a file to a different record. Narrow or deactivate them if that is more than the deployment needs."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:136
msgid "Cross-model side effects"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:138
msgid "A field can write into a model you never opened: ``crm.lead.email_from`` updates the partner's email. The gate refuses such fields, and the two crossings are not equally configurable. Where the crossing is static — a *related* field — the refusal is absolute and no access rule lifts it. Where it is dynamic — a field carrying an *inverse* — it is lifted only by a rule naming that exact field, so a model-level grant cannot leak sideways and the audit trail cannot silently under-report what a write touched. On the file-fetch path (``fetch_file_to_field``) the per-field opt-in does not apply at all: a target field carrying an inverse is refused whatever rule you write, because the content comes from a URL the model chose."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:149
msgid "Ten field-scoped write allows ship enabled on databases with **Accounting**, for the invoicing flow. Four are on ``account.move.line``, and on the nested one2many path a field-level Allow is resolved before the model tier, so those four line fields are writable through :guilabel:`Invoice lines` even when no rule allows writing ``account.move.line`` itself. Review them before certifying that invoice lines are closed. One of the four, ``analytic_distribution``, needs extra care: it is a JSON field, so its rule lifts the refusal of JSON-typed values as well as the cross-model rail, and its inverse re-writes the analytic entries of a **posted** line — deleting and re-creating ``account.analytic.line`` records on a model no rule named. See :ref:`ai/policy/cross-model`."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:161
msgid "Sensitive field stripping"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:163
msgid "Fields whose names look like secrets (password, token, api_key, …), magic fields, and certain complex types are stripped or refused on write. This is defense-in-depth, not a substitute for denying whole models (e.g. HR)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:168
msgid "Confirmation and re-check"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:170
msgid "Pending writes are re-validated at **apply** time under the correct acting identity and current policy. Approving an old proposal after rights were revoked should fail closed. Agent proposals rebuild authority from the **run**, not from the confirmer's privilege (the confirmer authorises; the agent executes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:176
msgid "The run's own liveness is part of that authority. A proposal from a run that was stopped on request, or closed by the stuck-run reaper because its worker died, is refused at apply time and can be approved by nobody: the reaper cannot tell a dead worker from a slow one, so a run somebody else declared over is never allowed to have its writes replayed. Proposals left by a run that ended on its own — *Done*, *Failed* or *Timed out* — stay approvable until housekeeping expires them."
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:184
msgid "Security roles and groups"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:187
msgid "Group"
msgstr ""

#: ../mnt/documentation/content/applications/productivity/ai/security.rst:189
msgid "**AI: User**"
msgstr ""

